<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hethum I</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1213">1213</a>–<a href="/years/1270">1270</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hethum I was king of the Armenian Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cilicia">Cilicia</a> from 1226 to 1270, founder of the Hethumid line of kings. He submitted to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> and travelled to their capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karakorum">Karakorum</a> in 1254 to secure an alliance against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluks</a>. The alliance brought gains in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, but the Mamluks devastated Cilicia in 1266, and Hethum abdicated in favour of his son Leo II in 1269.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hethum-i.jpg" alt="Hethum I" width="200" height="206" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: The original uploader was Maximus Rex at English Wikipedia. · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hetoum_and_Zabel.jpeg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Hethum I was the son of Constantine of Baberon and Princess Alix Pahlavouni of Lampron, and he founded the dynasty that bears his name, the Hethumids, also called the House of Lampron. His father had been regent for the young Queen Isabella of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a>. Isabella first married Philip, son of Bohemond IV of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antioch">Antioch</a>, but Constantine had Philip disposed of and forced her to marry his own son Hethum on 14 June 1226, making the couple co-rulers. They had six children, among them Leo II, Thoros, who was killed at the Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mari">Mari</a> in 1266 fighting the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluks</a>, and Sibylla, who married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemond-vi-of-antioch">Bohemond VI of Antioch</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Hethum was a major player in the shifting alliances around the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusader-states">Crusader states</a>, as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenians">Armenians</a> had ties with all sides. When the Mongol commander <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baiju">Baiju</a> attacked the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultanate-of-rum">Sultanate of Rum</a>, Sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaykhusraw-ii">Kaykhusraw II</a> called on Hethum for help, but Hethum delayed, probably judging the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> the greater threat. After the Mongols crushed the Seljuqs at Kösedağ and approached Cappadocia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cilicia">Cilicia</a>, he submitted to Mongol suzerainty and sent his brother Sempad to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karakorum">Karakorum</a>. There Sempad made a formal agreement with the Great Khan Güyük in 1247 that made Cilician Armenia a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> state. In 1245 the Sultanate of Rum had attacked Cilicia, angered that Hethum had handed Kaykhusraw's wife and daughter to Baiju.</p><p>In 1254 Hethum travelled through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongolia">Mongolia</a> to renew the agreement and met <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongke">Möngke Khan</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karakorum">Karakorum</a>. His companion Kirakos Gandzaketsi recorded the journey, and the narrative is important for its observations of Mongol, Buddhist and Chinese culture, geography and wildlife. Hethum urged other Frankish rulers to submit to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a>, but only his son-in-law <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemond-vi-of-antioch">Bohemond VI of Antioch</a> did so, around 1259. Armenian troops were with the Mongol army that captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> in 1258, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenians">Armenians</a> fought under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hulagu-khan">Hulagu</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleppo">Aleppo</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a> in 1260.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluks</a> rallied and defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-ain-jalut">Ain Jalut</a> in September 1260. Because of Hethum's support for the Mongols, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cilicia">Cilicia</a> came under increasing attack from the Mamluks under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baybars">Baybars</a>, who invaded in 1266. The outnumbered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenians">Armenians</a> were beaten at the Disaster of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mari">Mari</a>, where Thoros was killed and Leo was captured. The cities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adana">Adana</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarsus">Tarsus</a> and Ayas were assaulted, the capital Sis was sacked and burnt, thousands of Armenians were massacred and 40,000 were taken captive. Hethum ransomed his son by conceding territory to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egyptians">Egyptians</a>. In May 1268 Baybars overran the Principality of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antioch">Antioch</a>. Hethum abdicated in favour of Leo and lived out his life as a monk in a monastery.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Hethum I” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
